    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 031150\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n450 AM MST Thu Sep 3 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 12Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Isolated showers and thunderstorms will be possible across South \n  Central Arizona late this afternoon.\n\n- Tropical moisture will arrive into the region tonight into Friday,\n  further increasing rain chances mainly across the foothills and\n  high terrain areas east of Phoenix before chances shift into \n  Southwest Arizona and Southeast California this weekend.\n\n- Temperatures remain in a near to below normal category for the \n  rest of this week and into the weekend, resulting in widespread \n  Minor Heat Risk and localized areas of Moderate Heat Risk.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nLatest GOES-18 mid-lvl wv imagery and 500 mb streamline analysis \nreveals a broad trough of low pressure encompassing the Pacific NW \nand Intermountain West while a ridge of high pressure extends from \nthe Southern Plains into the southeastern U.S. Our forecast area is \nsituated between both of these features under a prominent fetch of \ndry SW flow aloft. Meanwhile, now Cat 2 Hurricane Marie is churning \nover the E Pacific, well southwest of the tip of the Baja Peninsula. \nMoisture advection is ongoing well out ahead of the main circulation \ninto N Sonora MX and beginning to encroach on southern AZ. Moisture \nwill continue to be transported nwd into central AZ with PWAT values \nexpected to climb to around 1.5-1.7\" later today through early \nFriday, however there is expected to be a well defined moisture \ngradient from E to W across AZ. Much drier air associated with the \ntroughing to the NW of our region will extend into SE California and \nsouthwest AZ through Friday which will really limit rain chances \nacross the western half of the forecast area until better moisture \narrives this weekend. NBM PoPs have continued to shift ewd with each \nsubsequent run and the main focus for shower and storm activity now \nlooks to be east of Phoenix, mainly in the foothills of Maricopa \nCounty and S Gila County. This aligns with the latest WPC ERO for \nDay 2 (Friday) with an eastward shift in the slight risk which \nmostly encompasses the TWC forecast area and clips S Gila County.\nNonetheless, CAMs still indicate a few pop up isolated showers and \nstorms in central Maricopa County including the Phoenix Metro area \nlate today through early Friday and again Friday afternoon/evening. \nSome storms could produce gusty wind and localized flash flooding.\n\nDue to the presence of troughing to our west maintaining 500 mb \nhghts within a 587-590 dam range, temperatures will be within a \ndegree or two of daily normals through the end of this week. Lower \ndesert highs will generally range from the upper 90s to around 105 \ndegrees each afternoon, following morning lows from the upper 70s to \nmiddle 80s. The West Coast trough will also promote a tighter mid \nlvl pressure gradient over southeast CA and western AZ which will \nlead to continued breezy to locally windy conditions across the \nImperial and Lower Colorado River Valleys. Gusts could reach upwards \nof 25-30 mph in the aforementioned areas and potentially as high as \n40 mph in far SW Imperial County.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nGlobal models are coming into somewhat better agreement with the \npattern evolution this weekend. Both the ECMWF and GFS show high \npressure strengthening over the southern Plains with the western \nperiphery of the ridge extending over E AZ and NM. This will cause\nthe subtropical jet to shift wwd beginning Saturday, setting up \nbetter moisture advection over southeast CA and southwest AZ. NBM \nPoPs are still not overly impressive, mainly in the 20-30% range \non Saturday, however the GEFS/GFS indicates a final slug of \nmoisture from Marie being pulled into southeast CA on Sunday with \nPWATs rising above 2.0\". Therefore we will have to monitor for \nheavier rainfall potential in portions of SE California including \nJoshua Tree NP on Sunday. Elsewhere, across southcentral AZ, rain \nchances look to be meager due to more extensive cloud cover and \nvery low instability. The latest WPC QPF field shows a local \nminimum in rainfall totals (<0.10\") in southcentral AZ including \nthe Phoenix Metro through this weekend. By early next week, there \nare large discrepancies in the ridge/trough positioning which will\ncomplicate the precipitation forecast, but there will still be \nadequate moisture across our region which will keep low end PoPs \nin place across eastern and southeastern AZ Monday and Tuesday \nwith potentially better precipitation chances arriving Wednesday.\n\nThe temperature forecast this weekend into early next week remains \nlargely unchanged, although there will likely be a gradual increase \nfrom near normal temperatures on Saturday to slightly above normal \ntemperatures Sunday into early next week as ridging strengthens over \nthe region. Depending on how strong the ridge gets, NBM temps could \nclimb a few more degrees in subsequent runs and it is not out of\nthe question that we could see lower desert highs approaching 110\ndegrees as indicated by the NBM 75th percentile on Monday and Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1150Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nThe main aviation concern this TAF period will be the possibility\nof VCTS/TSRA/VCSH this evening and during the overnight/early\nmorning hours on Friday. Winds will tend to favor diurnal trends\nwith a slower than usual turn to westerly flow, resulting in a \nwindow of southerly crosswinds at KPHX and KDVT later this \nmorning. Hi-res guidance is hinting at TS developing in the \nsouthern and eastern portions of the airspace during the late \nafternoon/early evening hours with perhaps some cells migrating to\nwithin the vicinity of the terminals. KIWA is the most likely \n(20-30% chances) to see VCTS, but nearby convection cannot be \nruled out for the other sites. However, chances are too low (<20%)\nto warrant inclusion of even VCTS at this time for KPHX, KDVT, \nand KSDL. Hi-res guidance is also hinting at some additional \nSHRA/TSRA developing during the overnight/early morning hours \nwithin the airspace. VCSH has been included in the TAFs for now, \nbut confidence is low in this occurring. The activity may stay to \nthe east of the terminals. FEW- SCT cloud decks around 8-12 kft \nare expected through the TAF period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nBreezy conditions, once again during the afternoon and evening, \nhours will be the main aviation concern. Winds this morning will \nbe aob 10 kt, with some variability being observed at times, \nmainly at KBLH. Flow will contain some degree of a westerly \ncomponent at KIPL while KBLH switches between southerly and \nsouthwesterly directions. Mostly clear skies will be common over \nthe region.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nIsolated to widely scattered showers and storms will be possible\nin southcentral AZ later today through Friday. Rain chances will \nexpand westward through this weekend, becoming focused over the \nwestern districts Saturday and Sunday. The best chances for\nwetting rainfall will be over the higher terrain areas of eastern\nand southeastern AZ. Afternoon minimum humidities will range from\n7-15% across southeast CA and between 20-35% for the remainder of\nthe region through Friday and will likely increase above 25% \nareawide this weekend. Overnight recoveries will range from poor \nacross the western deserts and good to excellent across the \neastern districts. Daytime winds will remain elevated out of the \nsouth to southwest, with the strongest gusts in Southeast CA and \nalong the Lower Colorado River Valley peaking between 20-30 mph \nduring the afternoon/early evening.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Salerno\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...Berislavich/RW \nFIRE WEATHER...Whittock/Salerno\n",
